\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 Market Economy Theory
\f0\b0\fs24 \

\f2\fs20 There are (and not surprisingly) differences between \'93markets as we know\'94 and \'93markets in theory\'94. One has been noticed already. In theory, the market is a transaction between the producer and the consumer; in real life, the two parties are, respectively, sellers and buyers or customers. But there is a more important difference. In real life, the seller is rarely a producer. He/she is both a buyer and a seller, that is, a 
\f1\b merchant
\f2\b0 . 
\f3\i A striking feature of the standard theory of the market is that it leaves out this crucial agent
\f2\i0 . I shall enter into a discussion of the theoretical legitimacy of this exclusion in the next Section. In this Section I shall concentrate on what the real life implications of markets are whose active agents are merchants. Before I do that, however, let me touch upon a matter of pedagogic interest. I mentioned above that merchants offer larger quantities at lower prices. It appears quite reasonable as well. In other words, the merchant as supplier is a \'93rational\'94 participant in the system. But then what is the nature of the \'93supply curve\'94?
\fs14 6 
\fs20 It will be seen to be 
\f3\i downward sloping
\f2\i0 . With also a downward sloping demand curve the intersection between the two may not easily take place, and if it does it will pose problems! It is not surprising therefore, that theory decides to eliminate merchants and sticks to the position that the supplier is the producer! 
\f3\i Be that as it may, let us note that the critical difference between the market in theory and the market in real life is that 
